~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
[EXPLAIN] What this step does
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Builds and honestly evaluates machine-learning models that predict how potently a molecule
  inhibits TgDHFR (pIC50, higher         = more potent) from its structure. The dataset has 859
  molecules with measured potency.
  Molecules must be converted to numbers before a model can use them. Several descriptions are
  compared:         Morgan fingerprints (ECFP4, 2048 bits).         A fingerprint is a long 0/1
  list saying which small substructures are present; a descriptor is a number such as
  molecular weight, polarity or number of rotatable bonds; '3D' descriptors also depend on the
  molecule's shape.
  For each description, the models tried are single algorithms (Random Forest, Extra Trees,
  LightGBM, XGBoost, SVM,         kNN) and stacks of up to 3 of them combined by a final Ridge
  regression. NESTED cross-validation         is used so the reported score is not optimistic: the
  data is split into 5 parts and the whole         split is repeated 5 times (= 25 outer folds).
  Inside every outer fold the training part is         split again (5 inner folds) to choose the
  best algorithm combination; only then is that choice         scored on the outer test part,
  which it has never seen. Selecting and scoring on the same data would inflate R2.
  This is by far the slowest step of the pipeline (each outer fold fits hundreds of models).
  Progress is saved after every         fold (results/01_nested_cv_checkpoint.csv), so an
  interrupted run resumes where it stopped. IMPORTANT: if the training         data changes,
  delete that checkpoint first or old-data folds would be silently reused.
  Per-fold line: 'Best' = the algorithm combination that won the inner selection; R2_outer =
  fraction of the variance in         potency explained on the unseen part (1 = perfect, 0 = no
  better than predicting the mean); MAE = average error in         pIC50 units (0.5 = about a
  3-fold error in IC50); the time is for that fold.
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

